# session_verification — an impure-boundary PORT for verifying a session
# credential, declared once and used by AuthIdentity's hexagon. Two-color
# rule : sync domain, async adapter, NEVER a lock.
#
# NAMED "session_verification", not "authentication" (parser-removal plan,
# Phase 1a, replacing the dead authentication.family — .family was never a
# parsed hecksagain construct, confirmed : *.family isn't even in the
# loader's DOMAIN_ORDER glob). hecksagain's own vendored library already
# ships an `authentication.port` (verb authenticated_by, signal :reply — a
# different, simpler synchronous-check concept). Registry#add_port is a flat
# name-keyed overwrite, and load_library runs before load_domain in the SAME
# boot (runtime/loader.rb) — reusing the name "authentication" here would
# silently clobber one port with the other. Distinct name, distinct registry
# slot, no collision.
#
# Signal is EFFECT (not reply), the exact shape of payment : AuthIdentity.Establish
# EMITS Established, the verifier adapter does its async external check (validate the
# token against the provider named by the credential), and the verdict RE-ENTERS the
# same domain as a command — MarkVerified on success, Reject on failure. The whole
# round-trip rides one how-verb — `verified_by` — hung off the aggregate FQN with its
# triggering event (`on: "Established"`).
#
# This is authn 'via adapters' WITHOUT the two-color violation : verification is a
# ONCE-PER-SESSION effect at Establish, never a per-dispatch blocking call. The
# per-dispatch authenticate Gate stays IN-PROCESS, reading the verdict this effect
# already recorded (AuthIdentity status active). The per-deployment VALUES (endpoint,
# token, timeout) live in .world, declared on the adapter that implements this port
# (SessionVerifier) — a port names verb/signal/produces only, never per-deployment
# config.
Hecks.port "session_verification" do
  verb     "verified_by"
  signal   :effect
  produces :verification_ref   # the verdict data a conforming handler emits on success
end
